@*
    Where this transfer has got to.

    The workflow has twelve durable states with names that mean something to the people who built it
    and nothing to a Windows administrator on shift. This collapses them into the five things the
    operator actually does, in order, and marks how far the record has got.

    Restrained on purpose: a line of steps, not a wizard. It does not drive the workflow and it does
    not enable anything — the buttons remain governed by authoritative state. It answers one
    question, which is "what has already happened here".

    Position comes from the record's durable state, so a browser refresh, a second operator, or a
    record picked up hours later all show the same thing.
*@

<ol class="so-steps" aria-label="Operasyon kaydı ilerlemesi">
    @for (int index = 0; index < OperationalRecordView.OperatorSteps.Count; index++)
    {
        bool done = index < Current;
        bool active = index == Current;

        <li class="so-step @(done ? "so-step--done" : null) @(active ? "so-step--active" : null)"
            aria-current="@(active ? "step" : null)">
            <span class="so-step-marker" aria-hidden="true">
                @if (done)
                {
                    <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Check" Size="Size.Small" />
                }
                else
                {
                    @(index + 1)
                }
            </span>
            <span class="so-step-label">@OperationalRecordView.OperatorSteps[index]</span>
            @* Read by assistive technology only; the visual state is carried by the marker. *@
            <span class="sr-only">@(done ? "tamamlandı" : active ? "sıradaki adım" : "bekliyor")</span>
        </li>
    }
</ol>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Zero-based index of the step the record is on; the step count when the transfer is finished.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int Current { get; set; }
}
